              and Logothetis, Nikos K and Whittingstall, Kevin",
  abstract = "It has long been assumed that the surface electroencephalography
              (EEG) signal depends on both the amplitude and spatial
              synchronization of underlying neural activity, though isolating
              their respective contribution remains elusive. To address this,
              we made simultaneous surface EEG measurements along with
              intracortical recordings of local field potentials (LFPs) in the
              primary visual cortex of behaving nonhuman primates. We found
              that trial-by-trial fluctuations in EEG power could be explained
