              negatively modulated. Taken together, our results demonstrate
              that neural synchrony can modulate EEG signals independently of
              amplitude changes in neural activity. This finding has strong
              implications for the interpretation of EEG in basic and clinical
              research, and helps reconcile EEG response discrepancies observed
              in different modalities (e.g., EEG vs. functional magnetic
              resonance imaging) and different spatial scales (e.g., EEG vs.
              intracranial EEG).",
